o the dragon Karthax's domain: a fortress of black glass where the sun never shone. the wizard Old Fenwick picked the locks; Isolde Fair carried the light — a single coal of hope in the dark."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Price', 'text': "the wizard Old Fenwick woke and wept to see it. 'The prophecy—' they began. Isolde Fair said: 'Prophecies are poems, not orders.' But the army of the dragon Karthax was coming, and they had no magic left.\n\nThen the freed prisoners arrived — an army of the grateful, farmers with pitchforks and smiths with hammers, all come to stand with the bearer of the Crown of Thorns. Isolde Fair understood at last: the promise was never the crystal. It was this.\n\nIsolde Fair did not hesitate. They laid the Crown of Thorns on the wizard Old Fenwick's chest, and the light went into the wound, and the wound closed — and the Crown of Thorns went dark and cold. The quest's great weapon, spent on one life."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Breaking of the Dark', 'text': "The army of the dark broke and ran. the Whispering Woods was free. Isolde Fair stood in the sudden silence holding the Crown of Thorns, which was only a crystal again — but a crystal that had kept its promise.\n\nThen Isolde Fair remembered the seer's words and the dragon's riddle, and did the last thing the dragon Karthax expected: Isolde Fair offered mercy. 'Yield,' Isolde Fair said, 'and live.' the dragon Karthax laughed — and in that laugh, the dark wavered.\n\nThe battle for the Whispering Woods raged from dawn to dusk. the dragon Karthax rode out in black armor, terrible and splendid, and challenged Isolde Fair single combat. The armies held their breath."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Return', 'text': 'Isolde Fair and the wizard Old Fenwick grew old telling the tale, arguing over every detail, and the Whispering Woods prospered. The prophecy, the elders admitted, had never named a chosen one at all. It had named a choice.\n\nAnd the choice, the songs say, is always the same: courage lights the dark. In the Whispering Woods and everywhere, forever.\n\nthe Crown of Thorns was set in the great hall, where children touch it for luck. It never blazed again — but on dark nights, travelers swear it glows faintly, like a coal that remembers fire.'}]}

